\begin{abstract}

% This work presents an 808-line Matlab educational code for combined multi-scale topology optimisation and phasor-based dehomogenisation titled \deHomTop. 
% The complete Matlab code can be downloaded from the website \url{https://bit.ly/49x08hT}.
% The dehomogenisation procedure is the method that allows for a coarse-scale optimization using optimal microstructure to obtain multi-scale structures that perform on par with large-scale topology-optimized structures. The multi-scale structure is reconstructed into a high-resolution single-scale structure with a minor loss in structural performance and at a fraction of the cost of the large-scale approach. This allows users to obtain high-resolution, near-optimal performing structures on a standard laptop.
% The multi-scale topology optimization uses the stiffness optimal Rank-2 microstructure to maximize the stiffness of a single-load case problem subjected to a volume fraction constraint. The multi-scale structure can be reconstructed to a single-scale using the phasor-based dehomogenisation method very effectively. The high efficiency of the phasor-based dehomogenisation allows for on-the-fly reconstruction. The dehomogenisation code can also be used separately for any lamellar-based multi-scale structure.
% The code finally allows for structural verification of the reconstructed structure with respect to the multi-scale structure.
% The work presents serval examples and extensions for the \deHomTop code.

This work presents an 808-line Matlab educational code for combined multi-scale topology optimisation and phasor-based dehomogenisation titled \deHomTopns. The multi-scale formulation utilises homogenisation of optimal microstructures to facilitate efficient coarse-scale optimisation. Dehomogenisation allows for a high-resolution single-scale reconstruction of the optimised multi-scale structure, achieving minor losses in structural performance, at a fraction of the computational cost, compared to its large-scale topology optimisation counterpart. The presented code utilises stiffness optimal Rank-2 microstructures to minimise the compliance of a single-load case problem, subject to a volume fraction constraint. By exploiting the inherent efficiency benefits of the phasor-based dehomogenisation procedure, on-the-fly dehomogenisation to a single-scale structure is obtained. The presented code includes procedures for structural verification of the final dehomogenised structure by comparison to the multi-scale solution. The code is introduced in terms of the underlying theory and its major components, including examples and potential extensions, and can be downloaded from \href{https://github.com/peterdorffler/deHomTop808.git}{Github}.

\end{abstract}

