\section{Conclusion}
In this work, we propose a self-refining framework that enhances ASR performance with only unlabeled datasets. We outline a methodology that leverages high-quality TTS-synthesized audio to bootstrap and enhance the performance of the original ASR system. Results show that our framework is effective in Mandarin and code-switching scenarios for both long-form and short-form audio, with performance gains up to 55.88\% compared to the original model \textit{Whisper-large-v2}. Compared to pseudo-labeling self-distillation approaches employed by \textit{Whisper-large-v3} and \textit{Cool-whisper}, our method achieves comparable or superior performance with significantly higher data efficiency. Overall, our framework addresses the limitations posed by real data scarcity and offers a generalizable solution for adapting ASR systems to low-resource and underrepresented speech domains.
%The proposed framework integrates key components such as audio filtering, forced alignment, awareness of both short- and long-form utterances enhancing their ability to handle time-aware and long-form transcriptions within specific domains, even under limited data conditions. Our results demonstrate that modern TTS systems enable the effective use of large-scale text corpora to compensate for the limitations of pseudo-labeling strategies commonly used in semi-supervised learning.

%Specifically, our method addresses several known challenges of pseudo-labeling, including the difficulty of collecting a large amount of domain-specific speech data, the frequent failure to correctly transcribe proper nouns, and cases where the model generates phonetically similar but semantically or orthographically incorrect transcriptions—i.e., phoneme-level correctness without lexical correctness.

%The proposed framework integrates key components such as audio filtering, forced alignment, awareness of both short- and long-form utterances, and mechanisms to preserve multilingual and code-switching capabilities while mitigating catastrophic forgetting. Focusing on Taiwan Mandarin and Mandarin-English code-switching as our target domains, we demonstrate that even with a relatively small amount of carefully synthesized data, our approach significantly improves ASR performance in scenarios where pseudo-labeling alone is insufficient, and this framework is adaptable to other low-resource or domain-specific scenarios. By utilizing large-scale text corpora in conjunction with TTS systems, the proposed method provides a generalizable path toward ASR adaptation in diverse underrepresented domains.

