\subsection{Phase 4: Regulated Assertion Generation}

Given a set of formal language statements
$\Phi=\{\phi_1, \phi_2, ...\}$, Phase 4 generates SVAs. SVA is chosen for its industry-wide adoption, rich temporal operators, and compatibility with formal tools such as JasperGold~\cite{JasperAppsCommand2024}
This phase iterates through $\Phi$ and processes one statement in each iteration,
which consists of three steps.

\noindent
\underline{Step 1: Formal statement pruning}. A statement $\phi_i \in \Phi$ is pruned if it does not contain any signals in the mapping table $M$ obtained in Phase 2 (Section~\ref{sec:semantic_regularization}.This pruning is not performed in Phase 2 because the results of Phase 2 are function description sentences, each of which may lead to multiple formal statements in Phase 3. In other words, a function description sentence containing a signal in $M$ may become multiple formal statements, where some of the statement no longer contain the signal. This pruning is performed through Python. The pruning significantly reduces the likelihood of generating assertions that contain non-existent or irrelevant signals. 

\noindent
\underline{Step 2: Formal statement decomposition}. Each remaining formal statement $\phi$ is decomposed into a pair of antecedent $\alpha$ and consequent $\xi$ by the LLM. In logic theory, {\bf antecedent} is the condition that triggers a behavior and {\bf consequent} specifies the resulting behavior. The decomposition preserves the semantic fidelity of the specification, ensuring that the intended behavior is not distorted during the SVA generation process. In addition, the LLM is instructed to present the antecedents and consequents in the format of \texttt{sequence} constructs in SystemVerilog. This format facilitates easy realization of Step 3. 

\noindent    
\underline{Step 3: Assembling to SVAs}. 
We use Python to assemble each pair antecedent $\alpha$ and consequent $\xi$ into an SVA. Since both $\alpha$ and $\xi$ are already in SystemVerilog format, this step becomes straightforward. 

\iffalse

\begin{algorithm}[ht]
\caption{Formal Language Description to SVA Transformation Pipeline}
\label{alg:sva_transformation}
\KwIn{
    Mapping table $\mathcal{M}$;\\
    Port list $\mathcal{P}$;\\
    LLM model $\mathcal{LLM}$;\\
    Formal language descriptions $\mathcal{F}$
}
\KwOut{SVA assertions $\mathcal{SVA}$}

$\mathcal{S}_{\text{ref}} \gets \texttt{ExtractReferencedSignals}(\mathcal{M}, \mathcal{P})$\;

Initialize $\mathcal{F}_{\text{valid}} \gets [\ ]$\;

\ForEach{$entry \in \mathcal{F}$}{
    \If{entry references any $s \in \mathcal{S}_{\text{ref}}$}{
        Append $entry$ to $\mathcal{F}_{\text{valid}}$\;
    }
}

\ForEach{$entry_i \in \mathcal{F}_{\text{valid}}$}{
   $block_i(antecedent_i, consequent_i) \gets \texttt{LLM}(entry_i)$\;
}

Filter blocks where both $antecedent_i$ and $consequent_i$ contain signals in $\mathcal{S}_{\text{ref}}$\;

\ForEach{valid block}{
    $\mathcal{SVA}$ =\texttt{FormatAssertion}
    $(antecedent_i, consequent_i,module_i)$\;
}

\Return{$\mathcal{SVA}$}
\end{algorithm}

Initially, the system extracts a set of referenced signals from the design using a mapping table and a port list. Formally, let $\mathcal{S}_{\text{ref}}$ denote the set of all signals extracted
\[
\mathcal{S}_{\text{ref}} \gets \texttt{ExtractReferencedSignals}(\mathcal{M},\mathcal{P})
\]
Subsequently, the complete set $\mathcal{F}$ of formal language descriptions is filtered to produce a validated list $\mathcal{F}_{\text{valid}}$, where each entry is retained only if it references at least one signal in $\mathcal{S}_{\text{ref}}$. This filtering ensures that the subsequent stages focus exclusively on specification components relevant to the design signals.

For every entry $e_i \in \mathcal{F}_{\text{valid}}$, a Large Language Model (LLM) performs a CoT reasoning. Specifically, the LLM maps each valid entry into a block consisting of:
\begin{itemize}[label={--}]
    \item \textbf{Antecedent} ($a_i$): The part that captures the pre-condition or trigger for a design behavior.
    \item \textbf{Consequent} ($c_i$): The part that specifies the expected behavior or result.
\end{itemize}
Both components are formulated as \texttt{sequence} constructs to align with the temporal semantics of SystemVerilog Assertions, enabling expressive and time-sensitive verification.

Each decomposed block is validated to ensure both its antecedent and consequent sequences reference at least one signal in the extracted signal set $\mathcal{S}_{\text{ref}}$. Formally, a block $(a_i, c_i)$ is retained only if:
\[
\texttt{Signals}(a_i) \cap \mathcal{S}_{\text{ref}} \neq \emptyset \quad \text{and} \quad \texttt{Signals}(c_i) \cap \mathcal{S}_{\text{ref}} \neq \emptyset
\]
This constraint ensures that the generated assertion is semantically grounded in the actual design context and does not reference irrelevant or non-existent signals.

If a block fails this validation criterion, the system re-invokes the LLM to regenerate the antecedent and consequent sequences from the original description. This re-generation is repeated until a valid and signal-aligned assertion block is obtained or a predefined retry limit is reached. This mechanism significantly reduces the risk of hallucinated signals and improves the overall correctness and applicability of the generated assertions.
For each validated block, the function \texttt{FormatAssertion} is applied to convert the $(a_i, c_i)$ pair into a SVA.

The final output is the complete set of formatted SVAs, denoted as $\mathcal{SVA}$:
\[
\mathcal{SVA} \gets \texttt{FormatAssertion}(a_i, c_i, \text{module}_i) \quad \forall\, \text{valid blocks}
\]
These assertions are then ready for use in formal verification environments.

This pipeline not only systematically bridges unstructured specifications and formal SVAs but also enables isolation of verification points, enhancing both interpretability and precision.

