\subsection{Limitations of Prior Work}

Existing assertion ranking methodologies, notably Pal et al.~\cite{palAssertionRankingUsing2020}, are primarily tailored for assertions with a \emph{single consequent signal}. While effective for simple one-to-one signal relationships, these methods inherently struggle to scale to more realistic verification scenarios, where assertions involve multiple consequential signals and capture richer behavioral constraints.

This limitation is partly rooted in the generation strategies employed by GoldMine~\cite{vasudevanGoldMineAutomaticAssertion2010}, which predominantly produce assertions involving individual signal bits, such as \texttt{tx\_outport\_req[3]} within \texttt{tx\_outport\_req[4:0]}. Consequently, Pal’s method lacks the mechanisms necessary to evaluate assertions with multiple interdependent consequents.

