\subsection{Results on Formal Verification of Assertions}

The generated assertions are submitted to JasperGold for formal verification. A formal verification failure for an assertion $\theta$ indicates that the design specification may be incomplete with respect to $\theta$. For instance, if the specification lacks sufficient detail about input conditions, JasperGold might explore a behavior space $\Psi$ that is either beyond its practical analysis capability or leads to unrealistic scenarios, cases that would not occur in actual operation. Therefore, a formal verification failure does not necessarily invalidate the assertion and the assertion may still function correctly in RTL simulations.
That said, for a given design specification, a higher number of assertions passing formal verification is desirable, as it reflects better coverage within the well-defined behavior space of the specification. As shown in Columns 8–10 of Table~\ref{tab:evaluation_normalized}, Spec2Assertion produces much more assertions that pass formal verification compared to GoldMine and AssertLLM.

