\subsection*{Abstract}

% \jose{refine} 
LLM-based Conversational AIs (CAIs), also known as GenAI chatbots, like ChatGPT, are increasingly used across various domains, but they pose privacy risks, \re{as users may disclose personal information during their conversations with  CAIs.} Recent research has demonstrated that LLM-based CAIs could be used for malicious purposes. %Malicious applications of LLMs have recently received attention, which have demonstrated capabilities suggesting that LLMs could be repurposed to perform harmful tasks. 
However, a novel and particularly concerning type of malicious LLM application remains unexplored: an LLM-based CAI that is deliberately designed to extract personal information from users. %
%
In this paper, we report on the malicious LLM-based CAIs that we created based on system prompts that used different strategies to encourage disclosures of personal information from users. We systematically investigate CAIs' ability to extract personal information from users during conversations by conducting a randomized-controlled trial with 502 participants. %we randomly assigned them to interact with these CAIs. 
We assess the effectiveness of different malicious and benign CAIs to extract personal information from participants, and we analyze participants' perceptions  
%
%analyze the personal information disclosed by participants during their dialogues with different malicious and benign CAIs to assess their effectiveness in extracting personal information as well as participants’ perceptions 
after their interactions with the CAIs. Our findings reveal that malicious CAIs extract significantly more personal information than benign CAIs, with strategies based on the social nature of privacy being the most effective while minimizing perceived risks. This study underscores the privacy threats posed by this novel type of malicious LLM-based CAIs and provides actionable recommendations to guide future research and practice.

%reveal the alarming ease with which LLMs can be manipulated to elicit sensitive information, as all malicious CAIs significantly increased disclosure compared to baseline designs. Of particular concern is the Reciprocal approach, which proved especially effective in extracting sensitive information while minimizing user privacy concerns. We further discuss the implications of these findings, highlighting potential harms, ethical considerations, and the critical need for robust safeguards to prevent misuse and protect user privacy. The code for this work are available at \url{https://anonymous.4open.science/r/Privacy_at_risk-F4C8/}.





