We finish this section with some notation and terminology used in this paper. In a graph $G$, the \emph{neighborhood} of a vertex $v$ is the set
$N_G(v):=\{u\in V(G)\setminus \{v\}\mid uv\in E(G)\}$. We let $\overline{N}_G(v):= V(G)\setminus (N_G(v)\cup \{v\})$.
The degree of a vertex $v$ in $G$, denoted by $d_G(v)$, is the number of vertices adjacent to
it, that is, the cardinality of $N_G(v)$.  We drop the
subscript $G$ when there is no ambiguity. The complement graph of a graph $G$ will be denoted by $\overline{G}$.
Given any two graphs $G$ and $H$, we say that $G$ \emph{contains} $H$, if $H$ is an induced subgraph of $G$.  A
\emph{stable set} is a set of mutually  nonadjacent vertices.




 For a vertex-subset $S$ of $G$,  $G[S]$
denotes  the subgraph induced by $S$ in $G$, and $G-S$ denotes  the subgraph $G[V(G)\sm S]$.
For any two vertex-subsets
$A$ and $B$ of $G$, we denote by $[A,B]$, the set of edges which has
one end in $A$ and other end in $B$.  The edge-set   $[A,B]$ is \emph{complete} or $A$ is \emph{complete} to $B$, if each vertex in $A$
is adjacent to every vertex in $B$. We say that $A$ is \emph{anticomplete} to $B$ if $[A, B]=\es$. The edge-set $[A, B]$ is \emph{special} if each vertex in $A$ is adjacent to at most one vertex of $B$, and vice-versa. We say that the pair \{$A$, $B$\} is \emph{graded} if  $A$ and $B$ are cliques  and $[A, B]$ is  special.

For a positive integer $k$, we simply write $[k]$ to denote the set $\{1, 2, \ldots , k\}$, and we simply write $i\in [k]$ if $i\in \{1, 2, \ldots , k\}$ and $i$ mod $k$.


 \medskip
The remainder of the paper is organized as follows.   In \cref{sec:use}, we give some crucial and useful results, and in \cref{sec:P1+2P2} we prove \cref{col-thm-0} and its consequences. In \cref{genprop}, we present some  important structural  properties of ($K_4-e$)-free graphs that contain a
$C_5$ and use them in the latter sections. Finally in \cref{sec:2P1+P3,sec:3P1+P2} we prove \cref{col-thm-2,col-thm} and their consequences by using related interesting  structure theorems (see \cref{main-thm-2,main-thm}).




\section{Some useful results}\label{sec:use}
 In this section, we give some results  which we use often in the later sections. We say that a graph $G$ is a \emph{good graph} if   $V(G)$ can be partitioned into three cliques, say $Q_1$, $Q_2$ and $Q_3$,
			such that $\{Q_1,Q_2\}$, $\{Q_2,Q_3\}$ and $\{Q_3,Q_1\}$ are graded.
Good graphs play  a crucial role in this paper, and we will prove that good graphs with clique number $\omega\geq 4$ can be colored using $\omega$ colors.  We begin with the following lemma.
\begin{lemma}\label{4clqsizlem}
    If $G$ is a good graph with $\omega(G)=4$, then $G$ is $4$-colorable.

\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
    Let $G$ be a good graph as in the definition.  First note that the size of a maximum stable set in $G$ is at most 3, and   the size of any clique containing  vertices both from  $Q_1$ and from $Q_2\cup Q_3$ (say) is at most $3$.  So since $\omega(G)=4$, we may assume that $|Q_1|=4$, $|Q_2|\leq 4$ and $|Q_3|\leq 4$.  Since $G[Q_1\cup Q_2]$ is the complement graph of a bipartite graph, $G[Q_1\cup Q_2]$ is perfect. Also since $Q_1$ is a clique with $|Q_1|=4$, clearly $\omega(G[Q_1\cup Q_2]) =4$. Hence $\chi(G[Q_1\cup Q_2]) = \omega(G[Q_1\cup Q_2]) =4$.  If $Q_3=\es$, then we are done; so we may assume that $Q_3\neq \es$. Likewise, we may assume that $Q_2\neq \es$.
    Next we claim the following:

   \begin{claim}\label{grad-1} If $|Q_3|\in \{1,2\}$, then $G$ is $4$-colorable. Likewise, if $|Q_2|\in \{1,2\}$, then $G$ is $4$-colorable.\end{claim}


 \no{\it Proof of  \cref{grad-1}}.~If $|Q_3|=1$, then we let $Q_3=\{u_1\}$, and if $|Q_3|=2$, then we let $Q_3=\{u_1,u_2\}$. Since $\chi(G[Q_1\cup Q_2]) =4$, there are four nonempty disjoint stable sets, say $S_1$, $S_2$, $S_3$ and $S_4$ such that $S_1\cup S_2\cup S_3\cup S_4=Q_1\cup Q_2$.
  Now since $u_1$ has at most one neighbor in each of $Q_1$ and $Q_2$, there exist an $i\in \{1,2,3, 4\}$, say $i=1$,  such that $\{u_1\}$ is anticomplete to $S_1$.  Also since $u_2$ has at most one neighbor in each of $Q_1$ and $Q_2$, there exists a $j\in \{2,3, 4\}$, say $j=2$,  such that $\{u_2\}$ is anticomplete to $S_2$. Now $S_1\cup \{u_1\}$,  $S_2\cup \{u_2\}$, $S_3$ and $S_4$ are   stable sets whose union is $V(G)$.
  Thus   $G$ is $4$-colorable, and we are done.  This proves \cref{grad-1}.
 $\sq$

By \cref{grad-1}, we may assume that $|Q_3|\in \{3,4\}$ and $|Q_2|\in \{3,4\}$. Next:

    \begin{claim}\label{grad-2}  If $|Q_3|=3$, then $G$ is $4$-colorable. Likewise, if $|Q_2|=3$, then $G$ is $4$-colorable.  \end{claim}

\no{\it Proof of  \cref{grad-2}}.~We let $Q_3 =\{w_1,w_2,w_3\}$. By \cref{grad-1}, we may assume that $Q_1 \cup Q_2 \cup\{w_1,w_2\}$ can be partitioned into four  nonempty disjoint  stable sets, say $R_1$, $R_2$, $R_3$ and $R_4$, such that $w_1\in R_1$ and $w_2\in R_2$. So $R_3\cup R_4\subseteq Q_1\cup Q_2$, and hence $|R_3|\leq 2$ and $|R_4|\leq 2$. If $\{w_3\}$ is anticomplete to $R_3$, then $R_3\cup \{w_3\}$ is a stable set, and hence $R_1$, $R_2$, $R_3\cup \{w_3\}$ and $R_4$ are stable sets whose union is $V(G)$, and hence $G$ is $4$-colorable; so we may assume that  $w_3$ has a neighbor in $R_3$, say $u$. Likewise, we may assume that $w_3$ has a neighbor in  $R_4$, say $v$.  Then since $Q_i$'s are pairwise graded, clearly $\{w_1,w_2\}$ is anticomplete to $\{u,v\}$, and $\{w_3\}$ is anticomplete to $(R_1\sm \{w_1\})\cup (R_2\sm \{w_2\})$. Thus for the same reason, there exists a $k\in \{1,2\}$  such that $\{w_k\}$ is anticomplete to $R_3$, say $k=1$.   Then  $(R_1\sm \{w_1\})\cup \{w_3\}$, $R_2$, $R_3\cup \{w_1\}$ and $R_4 $ are four stable sets whose union is $V(G)$, and  hence $G$ is $4$-colorable. This proves \cref{grad-2}. $\sq$

 \medskip
 By \cref{grad-1} and \cref{grad-2}, we may assume that $|Q_i|=4$ for $i\in \{1,2,3\}$.  We let $Q_3 =\{w_1,w_2,w_3,w_4\}$. By \cref{grad-2}, we may assume that $Q_1 \cup Q_2 \cup\{w_1,w_2,w_3\}$ can be partitioned into four  nonempty disjoint  stable sets, say $T_1$, $T_2$, $T_3$ and $T_4$  such that $w_1\in T_1$, $w_2\in T_2$ and $w_3\in T_3$. So $T_4\subseteq Q_1\cup Q_2$, and hence   $|T_4|=2$. We let $T_4=\{t, t'\}$. If $\{w_4\}$ is anticomplete to $T_4$, then $T_4\cup \{w_4\}$ is a stable set, and hence $T_1$, $T_2$,  $T_3$ and $T_4\cup \{w_4\}$ are stable sets whose union is $V(G)$, and hence $G$ is $4$-colorable; so we may assume that  $w_4$ has a neighbor in $T_4$, say $t$.   Also if $\{w_4\}$ is complete to $T_4$, then since   $Q_i$'s are pairwise graded, clearly $\{w_1\}$ is anticomplete to $T_4$, and $\{w_4\}$ is anticomplete to $T_1\sm \{w_1\}$,  and hence $(T_1\sm \{w_1\})\cup \{w_4\}$, $T_2$, $T_3$
 and $T_4\cup \{w_1\}$ are stable sets whose union is $V(G)$, and hence $G$ is $4$-colorable; so we may assume that $w_4t'\notin E(G)$.  Then  since   $Q_i$'s are pairwise graded, we see that $Q_3\sm \{w_4\}$ is anticomplete to $\{t\}$, and there exists a $p\in \{1,2,3\}$, such that $w_p$ is nonadjacent to    $t'$. We may assume (without loss of generality) that $p=1$.  Then $T_4\cup \{w_1\}= \{t,t', w_1\}$ is a stable set.   Now if $\{w_4\}$ is anticomplete $T_1\sm \{w_1\}$,  then $(T_1\sm \{w_1\})\cup \{w_4\}$, $T_2$, $T_3$ and $T_4\cup \{w_1\}$ are four stable sets whose union is $V(G)$, and hence $G$ is $4$-colorable; so we may assume that $w_4$ has a neighbor in $T_1\sm \{w_1\}$, say $u$. So since $|T_1|\leq 3$, we have $|T_1\sm \{w_1, u\}|\leq 1$. Then since $Q_i$'s  are pairwise graded, we observe that $\{w_4\}$ is anticomplete to $(T_2\sm \{w_2\}) \cup (T_3\sm \{w_3\})$,  $\{w_2,w_3\}$ is anticomplete to $\{u\}$, and there exists a $q\in \{2,3\}$ such that $\{w_q\}$ is anticomplete to $T_1\sm \{w_1, u\}$. We may assume (without loss of generality) that $q=2$.    Then clearly $(T_1\sm \{w_1\})\cup \{w_2\}$, $(T_2\sm \{w_2\})\cup \{w_4\}$, $T_3$ and $T_4\cup \{w_1\}$ are four stable sets whose union is $V(G)$, and hence $G$ is $4$-colorable. This proves \cref{4clqsizlem}.
\end{proof}

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
 \includegraphics[height=3cm]{fig-opt.eps}
\caption{Some graphs with $\chi=4$ and $\omega=3$.  }\label{fig-opt}
\end{figure}

	\begin{theorem}\label{lem-good}
    If $G$ is a good graph with $\omega(G)\geq 4$, then  $\chi(G)=\omega(G)$.

\end{theorem}



	\begin{proof}
    Let $G$ be a good graph with $\omega(G)\geq 4$ as in the definition.  We prove the lemma by induction on $\omega(G)$. If $\omega(G)=4$, then the lemma follows from \cref{4clqsizlem}. Let $\omega(G)=k\geq 5$. Suppose that the lemma holds for all good induced subgraphs $H$  of $G$  with $\omega(H)\leq k-1$.  Let $S$ be the maximum stable set of $G$. Then clearly $S$ is a stable set such that $\omega(G-S) =\omega(G)-1$, and hence $\chi(G-S)=\omega(G-S)$, by induction hypothesis. Thus $\chi(G)= \chi(G-S)+\chi(G[S]) = (\omega(G)-1)+1 = \omega(G)$. 	This proves \cref{lem-good}.
\end{proof}

We remark that the assumption that the clique number $\omega\geq 4$ in \cref{lem-good} cannot be dropped, and that the constant 4 cannot be lowered. Indeed, there is a good graph $G$ with $\omega(G)=3$ such that $G$ is not $3$-colorable. For instance, take $G\cong H_1$ (see \cref{fig-opt}). Then $\chi(G)=4$, $\omega(G)=3$ and clearly $V(G)$ can be partitioned into three cliques, namely $Q_1:=\{v_1,v_6,a\}$, $Q_2:=\{v_2,v_3,b\}$ and $Q_3:=\{v_4,v_5,c\}$,
			such that $\{Q_1,Q_2\}$, $\{Q_2,Q_3\}$ and $\{Q_3,Q_1\}$ are graded.

\medskip
We will also use the following known results.

\begin{theorem}[\cite{spgt}]\label{spgt} (Strong Perfect Graph Theorem) A graph $G$ is perfect if and only if $G$ does not
contain an odd-hole or the complement graph of an odd-hole.
\end{theorem}

 \begin{theorem}[\cite{Ju-Huang}]\label{JH-thm}
 If $\cal G$ is a given  near optimal colorable hereditary class of graphs where
	every   $G \in \cal G$ satisfies   $\chi(G)\leq \max\{c, \omega(G)\}$, for some  $c\in \mathbb{N}$, and if \textsc{Coloring} for $\cal G$ is solvable in  polynomial time  for every fixed positive integer $k \leq c-1$, then  \textsc{Chromatic Number} for
	$\cal G$ can be solved in  polynomial time.
\end{theorem}


\section{The class of ($P_1+2P_2, K_4-e$)-free graphs}\label{sec:P1+2P2}
In this section, we prove \cref{col-thm-0} and its consequences. First we prove the following.



\begin{lemma} \label{2K2K1bound}
If $G$ is a  ($P_1+2P_2, K_4-e$)-free graph with $\omega(G)\geq 3$, then $\chi(G)\leq \max\{4, \omega(G)\}$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
 	Let $G$ be a $(P_1+2P_2,K_4-e)$-free graph.  Let $K$ be a maximum  clique in $G$, and let $K:=\{v_1,v_2,\ldots,v_k\}$, where $k\geq 3$.  Let $C:=\{v_1,v_2,v_3\}$, and let

\begin{center}
 \begin{tabular}{ccl}
 $R_{i}$ &:=  & $\{u\in V(G)\sm C \mid N(u)\cap C=\{v_i\}\}$, for $i\in [3]$,\\
 $M$  &:=  & $\{u\in V(G)\sm C \mid N(u)\cap C=C\}$, and \\
 $M'$&:=  & $\{u\in V(G)\sm C \mid N(u)\cap C=\es\}$.
 \end{tabular}
 \end{center}Let $R:=R_1\cup R_2\cup R_3$. Note that $K\sm C \subseteq M$. Moreover the following hold, where $i\in [3]$:

\begin{claim}
	\label{K2K1part} $V(G)=R\cup M\cup M'$.
\end{claim}
\no{\it Proof of \cref{K2K1part}}.~If there is a vertex, say $u\in V(G)\sm (R\cup M\cup M')$, then there is a  $j\in [3]$ such that $uv_{j},uv_{j+1}\in E(G)$, and then $\{v_1,v_2,v_3,u\}$ induces a $K_4-e$. So \cref{K2K1part} holds.~$\sq$

\begin{claim}\label{K2K1Ri}
The set $R_i$ is   either a clique or a stable set.
\end{claim}
\no{\it Proof of \cref{K2K1Ri}}.~Since $\{v_{i+1},v_{i+2}\}$ is anticomplete to $R_i$ and since $G$ is ($P_1+2P_2$)-free, we have $G[R_i]$ is ($P_1+P_2$)-free. Also since $\{v_i\}$ is complete to $R_i$ and since $G$ is ($K_4-e$)-free, we have $G[R_i]$ is $P_3$-free. Since $P_3$-free graphs can be expressed as the union of (disjoint) complete graphs, we conclude that if $R_i$ is   not a stable set, then it must be a clique. This proves \cref{K2K1Ri}. $\sq$

\begin{claim}\label{K2K1Mclq}
	The set $M$ is a clique, and $R$ is anticomplete to $M$.
\end{claim}
\no{\it Proof of \cref{K2K1Mclq}}.~If there are nonadjacent vertices in $M$, say $u_1$ and $u_2$, then $\{u_1,v_2,v_3,u_2\}$ induces a $K_4-e$; so $M$ is a clique. Next if there are adjacent vertices, say $r\in R$ and $m\in M$, then we may assume that $r\in R_1$ and then $\{r, m,v_1,v_2\}$ induces a $K_4-e$; so  $R$ is anticomplete to $M$.  This proves \cref{K2K1Mclq}.~$\sq$

\begin{claim}\label{K2K1Riclqnbd}
	For any   $u\in R_i\cup M'$,  $N(u)\cap R_{i+1}$ and $N(u)\cap R_{i+2}$ are stable sets.
\end{claim}
\no{\it Proof of \cref{K2K1Riclqnbd}}.~If there are adjacent  vertices, say $a,b\in N(u)\cap R_{i+1}$, then $\{a,b,u,v_{i+1}\}$ induces a $K_4-e$; so $N(u)\cap R_{i+1}$ is a stable set. Likewise, $N(u)\cap R_{i+2}$ is also a stable set. This proves \cref{K2K1Riclqnbd}. $\sq$

\begin{claim}\label{K2K1per}
	We may assume that $R\cup M'$ is not a clique.
\end{claim}
\no{\it Proof of \cref{K2K1per}}.~Suppose that $R\cup M'$ is a clique. Then from \cref{K2K1part} and \cref{K2K1Mclq}, $V(G)$ can be partitioned into two cliques. Hence $G$ is the complement graph of a bipartite graph and thus $\chi(G)=\omega(G)$, and we are done. So we may assume that $R\cup M'$ is not a clique.~$\sq$




\begin{claim}\label{K2K1Rimaxclqsz}
   $\omega(G[R_{i}\cup R_{i+1}])\leq 2$. Moreover $\chi(G[R_{i}\cup R_{i+1}])\leq 2$, and for any $m\in M$, we have $\chi(G[R_{i}\cup R_{i+1}\cup \{m,v_{i+2}\}])\leq 2$.
\end{claim}
 \no{\it Proof of \cref{K2K1Rimaxclqsz}}.~We will show for $i=1$. Suppose to the contrary  that $\omega(G[R_{1}\cup R_{2}])\geq 3$. Then there are three vertices in $R_{1}\cup R_{2}$, say $a,b$ and $c$ such that $ab,bc,ca\in E(G)$. By \cref{K2K1Riclqnbd}, we may assume that $a,b,c\in R_2$. Then from \cref{K2K1Ri}, $R_2$ is a clique.  Thus since $\{a,b,c,v_2\}$ is a clique, we have $\omega(G)\geq 4$, and so $K\sm C\neq \es$. Let $u\in K\sm C$. If there is a vertex in $R_3$, say $a'$, then by \cref{K2K1Riclqnbd}, we may assume that $aa',ba'\notin E(G)$, and then $\{a',a,b,u,v_1\}$ induces a $P_1+2P_2$ (by \cref{K2K1Mclq}); so we have $R_3=\es$. Likewise, we have $R_1=\es$. Then from  \cref{K2K1per}, since $R\cup M' = R_2\cup M'$ is not a clique, we have $M'\neq \es$. Let $v\in M'$. Then from \cref{K2K1Riclqnbd}, we may assume that $av,bv\notin E(G)$. Then $\{v,a,b,v_1,v_3\}$ induces a $P_1+2P_2$ which is a contradiction. So $\omega(G[R_{1}\cup R_{2}])\leq 2$. This proves  the first assertion.

To prove the second assertion, since $R_{1}\cup R_{2}$ is anticomplete to $M\cup \{v_3\}$ (by \cref{K2K1Mclq}), it is enough to prove that $\chi(G[R_{1}\cup R_{2}])\leq 2$. If $R_{1}$ and $R_{2}$ are stable sets, then we are done. Also, if both $R_{1}$ and $R_{2}$ are not stable sets, then from \cref{K2K1Ri},  \cref{K2K1Riclqnbd} and from the first assertion, it is easy to see that $G[R_{1}\cup R_{2}]$ is an induced subgraph of a $C_4$, and hence $\chi(G[R_{1}\cup R_{2}])\leq 2$.
So   we may assume that $R_{1}$ is   a stable set and that $R_{2}$ is not a stable set. By \cref{K2K1Ri}, $R_{2}$ is a clique. Since $\omega(G[R_{2}])\leq 2$, we may assume that $R_{2}=\{p,q\}$.
Now from \cref{K2K1Riclqnbd}, $R_{1}\cap N(p)$ is anticomplete to $\{q\}$.  Then  $(R_{1}\cap N(p))\cup \{q\}$ and $(R_{1}\sm N(p))\cup \{p\}$ are stable sets, and hence the second assertion holds.~$\sq$

\begin{claim}\label{K2K1RiMcomcol}
$\chi(G[R_{\ell}\cup M'])\leq 2$, for some $\ell\in [3]$.
\end{claim}
\no{\it Proof of \cref{K2K1RiMcomcol}}.~Suppose to the contrary that for each $\ell\in [3]$, we have $\chi(G[R_{\ell}\cup M'])\geq 3$.  Since for $\ell\in [3]$, $R_{\ell}\cup M'$ is anticomplete to $\{v_{\ell+1},v_{\ell+2}\}$, $G[R_{\ell}\cup M']$ is $(P_1+P_2)$-free and hence $G[R_{\ell}\cup M']$ is a complete multipartite graph. Thus since $\chi(G[R_{\ell}\cup M'])\geq 3$, for each $\ell\in [3]$, $G[R_{\ell}\cup M']$ contains a $K_3$, say with vertex-set $\{a_{\ell},b_{\ell},c_{\ell}\}$. By \cref{K2K1Riclqnbd}, we may assume that $a_{\ell},b_{\ell}\in M'$. Next we  show that for each  $\ell\in [3]$, $R_{\ell}\cup M'$ is a clique, and we will show for $\ell=1$. Suppose to the contrary that there are nonadjacent vertices in $R_1\cup M'$, say  $u$ and $v$. Since  $G[R_1\cup M']$ is a complete multipartite graph, there are three stable sets which are mutually complete to each other, say $S_1, S_2$ and $S_3$ such that $a_1\in S_1$, $b_1\in S_2$ and $c_1\in S_3$. Then we see that there is a $j\in [3]$ such that $\{u,v\}$ is complete to $S_{j}\cup S_{j+1}$, and then $S_{j}\cup S_{j+1}\cup \{u,v\}$ induces a $K_4-e$ which is  a contradiction. So $R_{\ell}\cup M'$ is a clique for each $\ell\in [3]$. Hence from \cref{K2K1per}, we may assume that there are nonadjacent vertices, say $p\in R_1$ and $q\in R_2$. Then $\{a_1,b_1,p,q\}$ induces a $K_4-e$ which is a contradiction. So  there is an  $\ell\in [3]$ such that $\chi(G[R_{\ell}\cup M'])\leq 2$.~$\sq$

\medskip
From \cref{K2K1RiMcomcol}, we may assume that $\chi(G[R_1\cup M'])\leq 2$. Hence $\chi(G[R_1\cup M'\cup \{v_2,v_3\}])\leq 2$.
Now if $\omega(G)=3$, then $M=\es$ and so from  \cref{K2K1Rimaxclqsz}, we have $\chi(G)\leq \chi(G[R_1\cup M'\cup \{v_2,v_3\}])+\chi(G[R_2\cup R_3\cup \{v_1\}])\leq 2+2=4$, and we are done. So we may assume that $\omega(G)\geq 4$. Then $K\sm C\neq \es$, and let $u\in K\sm C$. Then from  \cref{K2K1Mclq} and \cref{K2K1Rimaxclqsz}, we have $\chi(G)\leq \chi(G[R_1\cup M'\cup \{v_2,v_3\}])+\chi(G[R_2\cup R_3\cup \{u,v_1\}])+\chi(G[M\sm (C\cup \{u\})]) \leq 2+2+(\omega(G)-4)=\omega(G)$. This proves \cref{2K2K1bound}.  \end{proof}






 \noindent{\bf Proof of \cref{col-thm-0}}.~Since every $(P_1+2P_2,K_3)$-free graph $G$ satisfies $\chi(G)\leq 3$ \cite{Rand-Thesis},  clearly $f(x)$ is a $\chi$-binding function for the class of ($P_1+2P_2,K_4-e$)-free graphs, by \cref{2K2K1bound}.
	To prove that $f$ is the smallest $\chi$-binding function for the class of ($P_1+2P_2,K_4-e$)-free graphs, we let $G_1\cong K_1$, $G_2\cong C_5$, $G_3\cong H_1$ or $G_3\cong H_2$  (see  \cref{fig-opt}), and for $\ell\geq 4$, we let $G_\ell\cong K_\ell$, then  clearly for each $\ell\in \mathbb{N}$, $G_\ell$ is  a ($P_1+2P_2,K_4-e$)-free graph such that $\omega(G_\ell)=\ell$ and $\chi(G_\ell) =f(\ell)$.   This proves \cref{col-thm-0}. \hfill{$\Box$}


\medskip
Bonomo et al. \cite{BCMSSZ} showed that \textsc{Coloring} for  the class of ($P_1+2P_2$, $K_4-e$)-free graphs is solvable in  polynomial time   for every fixed positive integer $k \leq 3$. This result together with \cref{col-thm-0} and \cref{JH-thm}  imply  the following.

 \begin{cor}
  \textsc{Chromatic Number} for
	the class of ($P_1+2P_2$, $K_4-e$)-free graphs can be solved in  polynomial time.
 \end{cor}

\section{Properties of  ($K_4-e$)-free graphs  that  contain a  $C_5$}\label{genprop}

In  this section, we prove some general structural properties  of a ($K_4-e$)-free graph  that  contains a $C_5$, and use them in the latter sections.


Let $G$ be a ($K_4-e$)-free graph.
Suppose that $G$ contains a  $C_5$, say with
 vertex-set $C:= \{v_1, v_2, v_3, v_4,$ $ v_5\}$ and edge-set $\{v_1 v_2, v_2 v_3, v_3 v_4, v_4 v_5, $ $v_5 v_1\}$. Then since $G$ is ($K_4-e$)-free, we immediately see that for any $v\in V(G)\setminus C$, there does not exist an index $i \in [5]$ such that $\{v_{i}, v_{i+1}, v_{i+2}\} \subseteq N(v)\cap C$. Using this fact, we     define the following sets: For $i \in [5]$, we let:
\begin{center}
 \begin{tabular}{ccl}
 $A_{i}$ &:=  & $\{ v \in V(G)\setminus C \mid N(v) \cap  C  = \{v_i\}\},$\\
 $B_{i}$ &:=  & $\{v \in V(G)\setminus C \mid  N(v) \cap C = \{v_i, v_{i+1}\}\},$\\
 $D_{i}$ &:=  & $\{ v \in V(G)\setminus C \mid N(v) \cap C = \{v_{i-1}, v_{i+1}\}\},$\\
 $Z_{i}$ &:= & $\{ v \in V(G)\setminus C \mid N(v) \cap C = \{v_{i-2}, v_i, v_{i+2}\}\},$ \\
 $X_i$ &:=  & $B_i\cup Z_{i-2}$, and\\
 $T $ &:=  & $\{ v \in V(G)\setminus C \mid N(v) \cap C = \es\}$.\\
 \end{tabular}
 \end{center}
 Further we let $A:= \cup_{i=1}^5A_i$,   $B:= \cup_{i=1}^5B_i$, $D:= \cup_{i=1}^5D_i$ and $Z:= \cup_{i=1}^5Z_i$. So $V(G)= C\cup A\cup B\cup D\cup Z\cup T$. Moreover  for each $i\in [5]$, the following hold:

\begin{enumerate}[label=  $(\mathbb{O}\arabic*)$, leftmargin=2.25cm] \itemsep=0pt
\item \label{DZ} {\it $D_{i}$ is a stable set, and $|Z_{i}|\leq 1$.}
 \item \label{X-matching}  {\it $\{X_i, X_{i+2}\}$ is graded. Likewise, $\{X_i, X_{i-2}\}$ is graded.}
\item  \label{bto}
 {\it $[X_{i}, A_{i}\cup A_{i+1}\cup B_{i-1}\cup B_{i+1}\cup (D\sm D_{i-2})\cup (Z\sm Z_{i-2})]$ is an empty set.}
\end{enumerate}



        \begin{proof}
      \ref{DZ}:~If there are  adjacent vertices  in $D_i$, say $x$ and $ y$, then $\{v_{i-1}, x, y, v_{i+1}\}$ induces a $K_4-e$; so $D_i$ is a stable set.
Next suppose to the contrary that there are vertices, say $z$ and $z'$ in $Z_i$. Now if  $z$ and $z'$ are adjacent, then $\{v_{i}, z, z', v_{i+2}\}$ induces a  $K_4-e$, and if $z$ and $z'$   are nonadjacent, then $\{z, v_{i-2}, v_{i+2},z'\}$ induces a $K_4-e$.  These contradictions show that $|Z_i| \leq 1$. So \ref{DZ} holds.  $\sq$



\smallskip
\noindent{\ref{X-matching}:}~If there are nonadjacent vertices in $X_i$, say $u$ and $v$ , then $\{u, v_i, v_{i+1}, v\}$ induces a $K_4-e$; so $X_i$ is a clique. Likewise, $X_{i+2}$ is also a clique.  So it is enough to show that $[X_i, X_{i+2}]$ is special. Now  if there is a vertex in $X_i$, say $x$ which has two neighbors in $X_{i+2}$, say $u$ and $v$, then $\{x,u,v,v_{i+2}\}$ induces a $K_4-e$; so each vertex in $X_i$ is adjacent to at most one vertex of $X_{i+2}$.  Likewise, each vertex in $X_{i+2}$ is adjacent to at most one vertex of $X_{i}$.  So $\{X_i, X_{i+2}\}$ is graded. This proves \ref{X-matching}.  $\sq$

\smallskip
\noindent{\ref{bto}:}~If  there are   vertices, say $u\in X_{i}$ and $v\in A_{i}\cup A_{i+1}\cup B_{i-1}\cup B_{i+1}\cup (D\sm D_{i-2})\cup (Z\sm Z_{i-2})$ such that $uv\in E(G)$, then \{$v_{i}$, $ v_{i+1}$, $u, v$\} induces a $K_4-e$. So \ref{bto} holds.
       \end{proof}








