\subsection{Structure Graph-Based Measures}

Petri nets are graph-based models consisting of nodes and edges. We introduce graph-based measures for analyzing Petri nets, using a directed graph \( G = (V,E) \), where \( V = P \cup T \) is the set of nodes, \( E = F \) is the set of edges.

\begin{definition}[Graph density]
For a graph $G=(V,E)$ with $|E|$ number of edges and $|V|$ number of nodes, the graph density is:
$D(G) = \frac{|E|}{|V|(|V|-1)}.$
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Graph diameter]
The diameter of a graph $G=(V,E)$ is the longest shortest path between two nodes:
$
\textrm{d}(G) = \max\limits_{u,v\in V} \{\text{min distance from $u$ to $v$}\}.
$
\end{definition}


