%To this end, we design a spatial-local audio-visual synchronization (SLAVS) encoder, as shown in Fig.~\ref{fig:sap}. Specifically, SLAVS encoder first projects the temporal video sequence and the corresponding temporal audio sequence into video embeddings $V = \{ {v_t}\} _{t = 1}^T,{v_t} \in {\mathbb{R}^{H \times W \times C}},\forall t$ and audio emebeddings $A = \{ {a_t}\} _{t = 1}^T,{a_t} \in {\mathbb{R}^{C}},\forall t$ by the video encoder (\textit{i.e.}, the tiny version of ConvNeXt \cite{liu2022convnet}) and audio encoder (\textit{i.e.}, 2D ResNet), respectively. Then, a spatial attention pooling (SAP) operation is used to align the spatial dimensions of each pair of $v_t$ and $a_t$. Note that although directly utilizing the global spatial pooling can also match the spatial dimensions of audio embedding and video embedding with each other, it ignores the spatial-local correlation between audio and video, which is not conducive to modeling the synchronization patterns between audio and video. Therefore, the SAP operation based audio-visual correspondence, \textit{i.e.}, different facial regions contribute differently in the corresponding audio \cite{ma2021contrastive} is devised to align the video information and audio information. Taking the alignment process of a single frame feature $v \in {\mathbb{R}^{H \times W \times C}}$ and the corresponding audio feature $a \in \mathbb{R}^C$ as an example, SAP first uses similarity calculation to generate the attention map ${V}2{A} \in {\mathbb{R}^{H \times W \times 1}}$ that each entry indicates the contribution of the corresponding patch of frame to the audio. Then, $v$ is normalized according to ${V}2{A}$ to get a weighted video feature $\bar v \in {\mathbb{R}^C}$ that has better audio-visual correlation with $a$. Finally, concatenation operation is employed to fuse $a$ and $\bar v$ to achieve the instant feature $x \in \mathbb{R}^{2C}$. The temporal instant feature sequences ${X} = \{ {x_t}\} _{t = 1}^{{T}}$ can be formed by sequentially performing the same spatial attention pooling operation on $T$ pairs of audio and video. 
%
%It is noteworthy that SLAVS encoder is pre-trained only on real audio-visual pairs in a contrastive learning manner, similar to \cite{ma2021contrastive}, which facilitates the learning of more robust audio-visual representations. The size of the input video frame is set to $128 \times 128$. For each audio, the log-mel spectrogram computed by the short-time Fourier transform is fed into the audio encoder. The dimension of both the extracted video embeddings and audio embeddings are $768$. 

