%\end{minipage}
\caption{Simulation -- $\mathbf{gradbar}$; $d=2$; $n=100$}
\label{fig:fig6}
\end{figure}


The above $f_{plain}(\x)$ utilizes squared magnitudes in the parenthesis and therefore slightly differs from the corresponding one used in theoretical considerations. The variant from (\ref{eq:practeq1a0}) is practically more convenient as the derivatives are ``\emph{smoother}''.
Its theoretical  analysis would  conceptually follow line by line the one we presented earlier for the non-squared magnitudes. It is just that some of the elegant closed form expressions would not be available which would then additionally burden numerical evaluations and precision of the obtained results. We skip redoing such an exercise and refer to Sections 4.2 and 4.3 in \cite{Stojnicphretreal24} for the obtained results for $d=1$ case are presented. One needs to keep in mind that the theoretical analysis assumes that the initializers overlap in measurement independent. This is not quite the case with the spectral initializers used in the above practical running of $\mathbf{gradbar}$. However, as the obtained results show, this effect seems practically rather unnoticeable. Also, the norm is taken to be smaller than 1 which may be viewed as existence of a prior knowledge of the norm. However, such viewing is not necessary. For example, one can rerun $\mathbf{gradbar}$  $\sim 1/\epsilon$ times (without effectively changing the complexity order) with $\sim 1/\epsilon$ different norms and ultimately obtain results virtually identical (basically in the $\epsilon$ vicinity for any constant $\epsilon>0$) to the ones presented in Figure \ref{fig:fig6}.








