\textbf{PQ-based Vectors in GPU's HBM}.  Since PQ can significantly reduce the memory footprint of high-dimensional vectors via lossy-compression, even an entry-level GPU such as NVIDIA V100 with 32 GB HBM   can accommodate all compressed vectors in its HBM for billion-scale datasets. In FusionANNS, we pin all compressed vectors in the HBM, avoiding extensive data swapping between GPUs and CPUs that is commonly experienced in previous GPU-accelerated ANNS systems~\cite{RUMMY,Faiss_GPU,GPU_Graph1}. Since in-memory indices still remain the benefit of the  replication mechanism for boundary vectors, FusionANNS can efficiently obtain all IDs of candidate vectors, and then sends these vector-IDs (excluding vectors' content) to the GPU for distance calculations. In this way, FusionANNS also eliminates the performance bottleneck caused by the limited PCIe bandwidth between CPUs and GPUs.  

\textbf{Raw Vectors on SSDs}. 
Unlike IVF-based SPANN that stores all posting lists on SSDs, FusionANNS only needs to store raw vectors on SSDs for re-ranking. Since the volume of raw vectors is almost 8 times smaller than that of posting lists, FusionANNS can significantly reduce the storage consumption. For each query, since only
the re-ranking process arises a few I/O
requests, FusionANNS can also  alleviate the I/O bottleneck of SSDs for concurrent queries. 

\textbf{Remarks.} Overall, our multi-tiered indexing approach is significantly different from previous hierarchical indexing techniques proposed by SSD-based or GPU-accelerated ANNS systems~\cite{SPANN,DiskANN,Faiss,RUMMY}. It can significantly reduce the storage footprints on HBM, main memory, and SSDs. It also significantly reduces the data volume transferred across SSDs, CPUs, and GPUs. This storage-saving and transmission-efficient approach effectively supports CPU/GPU collaborative filtering and re-ranking for fast and accurate ANNS.

%Consequently, FusionANNS significantly improves throughput while maintaining a low latency.



%Using the metadata stored in the CPU memory, we will no longer rely on the posting list structure, but can process each vector individually in the subsequent process. Therefore, when employing the PQ technique, rather than processing vectors in each posting list separately, we directly convert each vector in the dataset into compressed vector. Then, we maintain the compressed vectors of the dataset in the GPU HBM due to their compact nature. Finally, the raw dataset is stored on SSDs, providing access to the raw vectors to enhance the final query accuracy.

%This index preserves the index quality benefits derived from the replication strategy, while avoiding the issue of index amplification. Once the candidate posting lists are obtained by traversing the graph, we interact only with the vector-IDs, eliminating the need for transferring the entire posting lists between devices. Throughout the search procedure, only the re-ranking process necessitates a minimal number of I/O requests. The lightweight data transmission alleviates I/O bottlenecks during concurrent queries, fully leveraging the benefits of GPU acceleration. Consequently, FusionANNS significantly improves throughput while maintaining a low latency.

\subsection{Heuristic Re-ranking}
\label{subsec:4.2}

%\textbf{Problem formulation.} A significant challenge of the re-ranking process is determining the optimal point at which to stop once sufficiently accurate nearest neighbors have been identified. As illustrated in Figure~\ref{rerank_per_query}, different queries often require re-ranking a varying number of coarse-grained nearest neighbors to achieve the same level of accuracy. If the re-ranking process continues beyond the optimal point, it leads to unnecessary I/O and computational overhead, ultimately degrading system performance.

Since PQ causes an accuracy loss during distance calculations, a re-ranking process is usually required to refine the intermediate results reported by the GPU. As mentioned in Section~\ref{SPANN variant},   to achieve the same level of query accuracy,  the minimum re-ranking numbers for different ANNS queries usually vary significantly.  Thus, a static configuration of the re-ranking number may cause unnecessary I/O operations and distance calculations, or result in an accuracy loss. 

%Because different queries requiring re-ranking different numbers of vectors in coarse-grained results requirements, as shown in Figure~\ref{rerank_per_query}, a static configuration will result in unnecessary I/Os and computations for ease queries. 
To circumvent this problem, we propose a \textit{heuristic re-ranking} mechanism to minimize I/O operations and distance calculations.  The key idea is to set a relative large re-ranking number conservatively for high accuracy, and to terminate the re-ranking process immediately once the subsequent search is no longer beneficial for improving the query accuracy. To achieve this goal, we divide the re-ranking process into multiple mini-batches   and execute them sequentially. Each mini-batch contains the same number of candidate vectors.  Since all candidate vectors are sorted with their distances in ascending order, the mini-batch executed earlier usually has a higher possibility to identify more vectors that belong to the final top-$k$ nearest neighbours.  
Once a mini-batch is finished, we exploit a lightweight feedback control model to check whether subsequent mini-batches are beneficial for improving the query accuracy. 

To simplify the problem, we use a priority queue $Q$ (i.e., a max-heap) to maintain the current top-$k$ nearest neighbours. Initially, the max-heap is empty. For each mini-batch, we calculate the distances between the query vector and vectors within this mini-batch, and insert the vector whose distance is less than the current maximum distance in the max-heap. When a mini-batch is finished, we calculate the change rate of the max-heap according to Equation~\ref{eq:changerate}:
\begin{equation}
\Delta = \frac{|S_{n} - S_{n} \cap S_{n-1}|}{k}, 
\label{eq:changerate}
\end{equation}
\noindent where \( S_{n} \) and \( S_{n-1} \) represent the sets of vectors' IDs in the max-heap when the mini-batch $n$ and the mini-batch $n$-1 is just completed, respectively. $k$ represents the number of vectors maintained in the max-heap, i.e., the number of the final nearest neighbours.
We terminate the re-ranking process if the change rate of the max-heap for successive mini-batches is smaller than a given threshold $\epsilon$ continuously for total $\beta$ times. 



%Then, we define the change rate threshold-\( \epsilon \) and stability threshold-\( \beta \) to help us determine whether subsequent batches are necessary for improving query accuracy.



%If such a low change rate continues stably  

%do not change the current top-$k$ nearest neighbours continuously. 

%a key challenge is how to early terminate the re-ranking process when a query has identified all true nearest neighbors that satisfy the accuracy requirement. To circumvent this, we propose a \textit{heuristic re-ranking} that divides re-ranking phase into multiple batches and then re-ranks the vectors in the coarse-grained results sequentially by batch. During this process, we early terminate the re-ranking process based on the changes in global results. This heuristic approach is based on the fact that when the change rate in global results remains consistently low, it can be assumed that the query has approximately identified all of its nearest neighbors. 

%Specifically, we first maintain a global top-k priority queue $Q$. During each batch execution, we compute the true distances between the vectors within that batch and the query vector, and insert the vectors whose distance is less than the maximum distance in the priority queue. After each batch, we calculate the change rate of the global priority queue as in the following equation:

\begin{algorithm}[t]
\renewcommand{\thealgocf}{1}
\caption{\label{alg:heuristic} Heuristic Re-ranking}
  \SetInd{0.1em}{1.5em}
    \KwIn{$Tasks,BatchSize,k,\epsilon, \beta$}
    \KwOut{$Q$}
    $Initialize\ Q \gets \textit{NULL}$

    $Initialize\ StabilityCounter \gets 0$

    \For{$i = 0; i \textless Tasks.size(); i += BatchSize$}
    {

        $S_{n-1} \gets Q.GetVectorIDs()$

        \For{$j = i; j \textless i + BatchSize; j ++$}
        {

            \tcc{Tasks[j] involves I/Os and computations}

            $Candidate\_Vector \gets GetDistance(Tasks[j])$

            $Q.insert(Candidate\_Vector)$

        }

        $S_{n} \gets Q.GetVectorIDs()$

        $\Delta \gets \frac{|S_{n} - S_{n} \cap S_{n-1}|}{k}$ \tcc{Calculating change rate $\Delta$}

        \eIf{$\Delta \leq \epsilon$}
        {

            $StabilityCounter \gets StabilityCounter + 1$

		\If{$StabilityCounter \geq \beta$}{



                \Return{Q} \tcc{Terminate re-ranking}
		}
        }
        {
            $StabilityCounter \gets 0$
        }
    }
    \Return{Q}
    \vspace{-1ex}
\end{algorithm}



