\end{figure}

\textbf{Optimized Storage Layout.} Although the vectors requiring re-ranking are obtained by PQ distances, they are all highly similar to the query vector, allowing them usually are spatially close to each other. This similarity offers an opportunity to mitigate the read amplification by carefully organizing the data layout on SSDs. Specifically, when the in-memory indexes are created offline, for each centroid in the navigation graph, we  use a bucket to store a number of raw vectors that are closest to the centroid. We note that there are not duplicate vectors among buckets. For each bucket, if it does not align with SSD pages, we combine buckets based on the size of unaligned portions using a max-min algorithm~\cite{max-min} to minimize the free space on a SSD page. Finally, We group all buckets as a single file and store it on SSDs, and use a table in memory to maintain the mappings between  vectors and SSD pages. 

% 我们使用一个uint32整数类型的数组结构来存储映射表，其中数组的索引表示向量的ID，而索引对应的值表示页号

\textbf{Intra- and Inter- Mini-batch I/O Deduplication.} Empowered by the optimized data layout, we design two I/O deduplication mechanisms, including merging I/Os mapped to the same SSD page within a mini-batch, and exploiting the DRAM buffer to eliminate redundant I/Os in subsequent mini-batches. Here, we use a simple example to describe
these mechanisms, as shown in Figure~\ref{Reordered Index}. Assume that there are two mini-batches in the re-ranking process, where the tasks of \textit{mini-batch 0} is to re-rank vectors: \textit{V2}, \textit{V4}, and \textit{V6}. The tasks of \textit{mini-batch 1} is to re-rank vectors:\textit{V5}, \textit{V8}, and \textit{V9}. When \textit{mini-batch 0} is executed, it first consults the mapping table to obtain the SSD page-IDs corresponding to the requested vectors. Since both \textit{V2} and \textit{V6} are stored in the same SSD page \textit{P0}, we can merge these two I/O requests and only read one SSD page to get \textit{V2} and \textit{V6}. Since \textit{P0} and \textit{P2} do not exist in the DRAM buffer, we directly read them to the DRAM buffer via two I/O requests. In \textit{mini-batch 1}, although \textit{V5}, \textit{V8}, and \textit{V9} are stored in different SSD pages, the DRAM buffer already contains \textit{P2} which includes \textit{V5}. Therefore, \textit{mini-batch 1} only needs to read \textit{P1} and \textit{P3} via two I/O requests.

%映射表，缓存，直接I/O
\textbf{Remarks.} We optimize the data layout on SSDs to enable intra- and inter-Mini-batch  I/O deduplication mechanisms, which eventually mitigate the effect of read amplification and  improve I/O efficiency.


%We use \textit{Direct I/O} instead of \textit{Buffered I/O} because (i) \textit{Direct I/O} offers higher I/O performance and (ii) the page cache for \textit{Buffered I/O} would be polluted by concurrent queries.

%对于查询过程需要的PQ相关计算，我们让每个GPU线程负责向量的每个维度的计算，从而最大化计算的并行度。在索引构建过程中，复制策略使得posting lists之间会存在重复的向量。为了确保查询结果的准确性，需要对这些候选向量进行去重。哈希算法可以检查数组中是否有重复的向量ID，但是利用GPU多线程并发地执行会导致结果错误。具体而言，当每个GPU线程负责一个单独的向量ID时，如果两个相同的向量ID对应的线程同时访问哈希表中相同位置的哈希值，并且该哈希值在此之前为0，那么这两个向量ID都会认为没有重复，最终都未被过滤掉。FusionANNS设计了一个支持并发去重的GPU内核。该内核通过一个自旋锁来保证同一时间只能有一个GPU线程访问和赋值哈希表中哈希值。当一个线程处理一个向量ID时，通过哈希算法计算得到对应的哈希表位置。然后，访问自旋锁获取该位置的访问权限，以确保没有其他线程正在访问。这种方式充分地发挥了GPU多线程的并行优势，同时保证了结果的正确性。

\section{Implementation}
We implement the system prototype of FusionANNS using 22K lines of codes in C++ and CUDA. FusionANNS can be widely deployed in general-purpose servers equipped with an entry-level GPU. %heterogeneous computing architecture composed of CPUs, GPU, and SSDs. 
Like most ANNS systems~\cite{SPFresh,SPANN,DiskANN,Starling }, we use each CPU thread to handle an individual query. 


%Similar to most ANNS systems, we use each CPU thread to handle an individual vector query. FusionANNS also supports multiple GPUs to handle extremely large datasets when PQ-based vectors cannot be fully accommodated in the HBM of a single GPU. In such cases, we distribute PQ-based vectors in multiple GPUs while maintaining their addresses in the host memory. Upon a query, the CPU sends candidate vector-IDs to the corresponding GPUs. Then, multiple GPUs simultaneously perform distance calculations, and return intermediate results to CPUs for accumulation.

\textbf{Contention-free GPU Memory Management}. FusionANNS implements a GPU memory manager specifically for concurrent ANNS queries. During system initialization, we first load compressed vectors into GPU's HBM,  and use the remaining space as a memory pool. Then, we divide the memory pool into several independent blocks, each of which is assigned to a single query as working memory. Once a query is finished, its block can be assigned to other pending queries. This approach can avoid frequent memory allocations and lock contention between queries, improving the system performance.


% \textbf{Contention-free GPU Memory Management}. During concurrent queries, frequent allocations and releases of GPU memory by multiple queries can lead to significant overhead due to lock contention. FusionANNS introduces a runtime GPU memory manager specifically designed for concurrent vector queries processing. The core of this manager is based on "pre-allocate" and "re-assign" strategies. At system startup, we first load the PQ vectors into GPU memory, then allocate the remaining space as a memory pool. This memory pool is divided into several independent blocks, each capable of meeting the GPU memory needs of individual queries. During runtime, each query is assigned to a free block with its intermediate data stored within that block. Once a query is completed, its corresponding block is marked as "available." This approach effectively avoid frequent interface calls and lock contention between queries, thus enhancing the system's concurrency.

\textbf{Efficient GPU Kernels}.
For each vector, we allocate multiple GPU threads according to their dimensions to calculate distances in parallel.
%We allocate a GPU thread for each dimension of query vectors to generate its distance table in parallel. Similarly, we also use a  GPU thread for each dimension of compressed vectors to calculate their distances in parallel. 
Moreover, we design a kernel to support parallel deduplication of vector-IDs using a hash algorithm. For a list of candidate Vector-IDs, we allocate a GPU thread for each vector-ID and use a spinlock to ensure that only one thread can access and update a hash table entry at a time. This approach can fully exploit  GPU's high parallelism to accelerate deduplication.

% \textbf{Efficient GPU Kernels}.
% For PQ-related computations during searches, each GPU thread handles  the computation of each vector dimension, maximizing parallelism. During index construction, the replication strategy result in duplicated vectors across posting lists. To ensure accurate results, the candidate vectors of each query must be de-duplicated. while a hash algorithm can detect duplicate vector-IDs within an array, concurrent execution by multiple GPU threads may lead to errors. Specifically, if two threads with the same vector-ID simultaneously access the same hash value, and it was initially zero, both threads might incorrectly assume no duplication. We designs a GPU kernel that supports concurrent de-duplication using a spinlock. This spinlock ensures that only one thread can access and update a hash table entry at a time. When a thread processes a vector-ID, it calculates its position in the hash table using a hash algorithm, then uses the spinlock to acquire the permission for accessing to that position, ensuring no other thread is accessing it simultaneously. This approach fully leverages the parallelism while maintaining result accuracy.

%\textbf{Overlapped pipeline}. We partition a query into multiple phases, and adjust the execution order of operations within each phase to create a parallel query pipeline. Initially, the CPU asynchronously transfers query vector to the GPU and invokes the GPU kernel to build the PQ distance table, while simultaneously traversing the graph index. Once the GPU begins filtering out irrelevant vectors, FusionANNS utilizes the CPU to riskily check the local cache in the order of the candidate vectors and compute the distances. Consequently, the subsequent re-ranking process can omit computations for vectors that are cached and not filtered out.

