\subsection{Scalability}
The throughput of ANNS systems is highly correlated to the number of CPU threads. %When more threads are used to serve concurrent queries, most ANNS systems would achieve a higher throughput, but experience higher latency per query due to resource competition. 
To evaluate the performance scalability of different ANNS systems, we increase the number of threads exponentially by a factor of 2.

%A common observation is that while increasing the number of threads can improve the overall throughput, the latency of individual queries often increases due to resource competition between queries. We evaluate the scalability of ANNS systems by using a power-law increase in the number of threads.

Figure~\ref{Scalability} shows the QPS and latency of different ANNS systems using different numbers of threads. FusionANNS show a significant growth in QPS when the number of threads increases from 1 to 64. For all ANNS systems, the QPS is almost the same when only one thread is used. However, when the number of threads increases to 64, FusionANNS significantly improves QPS by up to 13.2~\(\times\), 3.8~\(\times\), and 5.1~\(\times\) while remaining low latency, compared with SPANN, DiskANN, and RUMMY, respectively. SPANN achieves its peak QPS using only 4 threads, and its latency increases significantly with more threads. %DiskANN and RUMMY exhibit similar trends, with their QPS approaches saturation when the number of threads exceeds 8. 
Notably, for SIFT1B and SPACEV1B datasets, the QPS of RUMMY peaks at 16 threads, and then decreases distinctly. Meanwhile, the latency of RUMMY also significantly increases when the number of threads becomes larger than 16.   This reason of such limited scalability is that more concurrent queries arise a large amount of data transmission between CPUs and the GPU, which lead to significant PCIe bandwidth contention. Due to the bigger vector size of the DEEP1B dataset,
RUMMY suffers from more severe bandwidth contention, and thus its QPS is even lower than DiskANN, as shown in Figure~\ref{Scala:QPSDEEP}.
FusionANNS shows much better scalability than others even using limited memory resource because it eliminates the data swapping between CPUs and the GPU, and also significantly improves the I/O efficiency on SSDs.  

%This reason is that concurrent queries transfer a large amount data at the same time causing transfer blocking. What's more, due to the bigger vector size of DEEP1B dataset, the QPS of RUMMY only follows a similar variation trend to DiskANN, as shown in Figure~\ref{Scala:QPSDEEP}.

%FusionANNS remains low latency when the number of threads increases. Although other ANNS systems are able to maintain low latency when using a small number of threads, the latency increases significantly as the number of threads increases. In particular, SPANN has a nearly linear rising trend of latency when using more than 4 threads. These results demonstrate that even though FusionANNS uses limited memory resources, it still achieves excellent scalability.

