\section{Experiments on ARM Machine}\label{appendix:arm}
In addition to the x86 machine that we perform our main experiments on, we run the comparison on an ARM machine as well.
In this section, we give an overview over the results.
We use an Ampere Altra Q80-3 processor with 80 cores and a base clock speed of 3.00GHz.
The sizes of the L1 and L2 data caches are 64 KiB and 1 MiB per core, and the L3 cache has a size of 32 MiB.
The machine runs Rocky Linux 9.4 with Linux 5.14.0.
We use the GNU C++ compiler version 14.2.0 with optimization flags \texttt{-O3 -march=native}.
For the competitors written in Rust, we compile in release mode with \texttt{target-cpu=native}.
Because the machine does not support AVX instructions, we do not include SIMDRecSplit in our evaluation.
There are no competitors that specifically support the vector instructions on ARM machines, but the compiler might still automatically vectorize parts of all competitors.
\Cref{fig:paretoArm} gives our main comparison on the ARM machine.

