\begin{abstract}
  Generating high-quality, photorealistic textures for 3D human avatars remains a fundamental yet challenging task in computer vision and multimedia field. However, real paired front and back images of human subjects are rarely available with privacy, ethical and cost of acquisition, which restricts scalability of the data. Additionally, learning priors from image inputs using deep generative models, such as GANs or diffusion models, to infer unseen regions such as the human back often leads to artifacts, structural inconsistencies, or loss of fine-grained detail. To address these issues, we present SMPL-GPTexture (skinned multi-person linear model - general purpose Texture), a novel pipeline that takes natural language prompts as input and leverages a state-of-the-art text-to-image generation model to produce paired high-resolution front and back images of a human subject as the starting point for texture estimation. Using the generated paired dual-view images, we first employ a human mesh recovery model to obtain a robust 2D-to-3D SMPL alignment between image pixels and the 3D model's UV coordinates for each views. Second, we use an inverted rasterization technique that explicitly projects the observed colour from the input images into the UV space, thereby producing accurate, complete texture maps. Finally, we apply a diffusion-based inpainting module to fill in the missing regions, and the fusion mechanism then combines these results into a unified full texture map. Extensive experiments shows that our SMPL-GPTexture can generate high resolution texture aligned with user's prompts. 
\end{abstract}


\section{Introduction}
The generation of high‐quality, photorealistic textures for 3D human avatars is a critical problem in computer vision and multimedia field, with immediate applications in virtual try-on, film animation, gaming avatars, and telepresence, or solving occlusions given single-view input. Traditional approaches typically rely on 360° multi-camera setups or full-body 3D scanning to produce accurate textures, which requires dedicated effort in time and cost. Therefore, obtaining such a dataset can be expansive and additionally can raise ethical, privacy, and copyright concerns these datasets are from actual humans. 

Recent advancements have explored single-view methods, leveraging widely accessible 2D images from online or open-source databases to estimate complete UV textures for skinned multi-person linear model (SMPL) or SMPL‑X models \cite{loper2023smpl,pavlakos2019expressive}. These methods typically use convolutional neural networks (CNNs) or attention-based architectures to directly regress or 'inpaint' colour information onto the UV space. Nevertheless, relying solely on a single frontal view inevitably limits the capture of global context, resulting in incomplete or incorrect textures that lack critical appearance details from the subject’s backside.
To address this limitation, incorporating additional views, such as explicitly modeling the back view, has proven crucial for generating more accurate and artifact-free textures. However, real paired front and back images of human subjects are rarely available due to scalability challenges. Alternatively, generating unseen viewpoints using learned priors, diffusion, or inpainting techniques, may also lead to visual artifacts, such as blurring or inconsistent textures, particularly in occluded or unseen areas \citep{knuthwebsite}.

To address these issues, we present SMPL-GPTexture (SMPL- general purpose texture), a novel framework that leverages the latest text-to-image generation model to generate paired front and back images of a human subject as the starting point for texture estimation, as shown in Figure~\ref{fig:model}. Recent advances in text-to-image generation models, including GPT-4o \cite{openai2025gpt4oaddendum} have demonstrated strong capabilities of generating consistent and highly photorealistic human images while offering controllability through natural language prompts. By generating two complementary high-quality views, our approach overcomes the occlusion or inference ambiguities inherent in other methods and provides a more complete basis for texture reconstruction. 

With the paired front and back input images generated by the proposed text prompts, SMPL-GPTexture employs a state-of-the-art human mesh recovery (HMR) model to obtain a robust SMPL/SMPL‑X 2D fit for each view, ensuring a precise one-to-one correspondence between image pixels and the 3D model’s UV coordinates. It then utlizes an inverted rasterization technique that explicitly projects the observed colour from the front and back images into the UV space, thereby producing high resolution (1024x1024), accurate, complete texture maps. In the subsequent stage, a diffusion-based inpainting module fills in the missing regions, and a fusion mechanism combines these results into a unified, high-quality texture map.

SMPL‑GPTexture offers several notable advantages. First, by relying solely on natural language prompts for data synthesis without using real or online images for training or inference, our framework avoids the privacy, ethical, and cost concerns. Second, generating paired front and back views allows the model to explicitly capture front and back-facing information at the same time, leading to better reconstruction of occluded regions compared to approaches that either rely on single-view input, infer back views from learned priors, or require full multi-view setups.

