\section{Related Work}
{\bf Single-view and multi-view texture completion} Existing approaches to 3D human texture estimation have addressed the missing backside issue in different ways. Alldieck et al. \cite{alldieck2018videobased} fit a 3D SMPL model using monocular video and project each frame’s pixels onto the model’s UV map, blending these partial textures into a complete 360° representation. Zhao et al. \cite{zhao2020human} enforce cross-view consistency by predicting a texture from one view and then rendering it from an alternative perspective; the rendered image is then compared to the corresponding view using human-part segmentation cues. Xu et al. \cite{xu20213d} adopt transformer models where only a single image is used to infer the unseen backside, resulting in a unified texture. More recently, SiTH \cite{siTH2023} employs a diffusion-based pipeline to hallucinate a back-view image conditioned on the observed front photo, while ConTex-Human \cite{contexHuman2024} introduces a back-view synthesis module guided by estimated depth and a text prompt to generate a consistent backside texture. Although these methods primarily aim to enhance 3D texture reconstruction for SMPL-based models, they either depend on multi-view setups or suffer from artifacts when inpainting unobserved regions. 

{\bf Text-to-image models in texture generation} Recent advances in text-to-image generative models have enabled high-quality texture synthesis to align with users' requirements. AvataCLIP~\cite{avatarclip} leverages a shape VAE~\cite{shapevae} to initialize 3D geometry, followed by text-guided volume rendering refinement via CLIP~\cite{clip}. However, the generated textures often remain coarse, and the reliance on CLIP features can lead to imprecise or semantically entangled guidance. TexDreamer~\cite{texdreamer} generates high-resolution human UV textures by fine-tuning a text-to-image latent diffusion model~\cite{stable_diffusion} using text-texture pairs. Then it leverages large language models like ChatGPT~\cite{chat_gpt} to create 50,000 diverse, structured descriptions that guide the texture synthesis process by the fine-tuned diffusion model. CPO~\cite{cpo} leverages ChatGPT to generate positive and negative contrastive prompts, which are used to train a reward model that strengthens the alignment between long, complex user prompts and the resulting generated textures.

{\bf Summary on publicly / open database of people images / texture packs for 3d models.} 
Several publicly available datasets have been leveraged for SMPL-based texture estimation, and these can be broadly categorized into three groups. First, in-the-wild image datasets—such as pedestrian re-identification datasets (e.g., \cite{market1501}) and in-shop clothes retrieval datasets (\cite{deepfashion}), as well as multi-view video datasets—provide abundant images with varied viewpoints. However, these data sources offer only weak supervision and lack ground truth UV textures. Second, ground-truth scanned 3D human datasets (e.g., THuman2.0 \cite{thuman2.0}) comprise high-quality 3D scans of people with diverse clothing, together with accurately textured SMPL meshes. Although these datasets deliver precise texture information, their scale is typically limited due to the considerable expense and resource demands of the scanning process. Third, large-scale multi-view image datasets, such as MVHumanNet \cite{mvhumannet}, have been recently introduced. MVHumanNet, for example, collected over 645 million frames covering 4,500 identities from 48 cameras (yielding 9,000 outfits) and also provides textual descriptions of each sequence’s clothing, which is beneficial for validating text-to-image generation pipelines. However, these datasets are scarce and expensive to acquire, which motivates the need for efficient, synthetic dual-view methods to overcome these limitations.


\section{Methodology}

