\subsection{Dual-View Image Generation via Text-to-Image Models}

Figure~\ref{fig:prompt} illustrates a structured text-to-image prompting process for generating full-body, photo-realistic human images using predefined attribute and conditioned templates. On the left, a set of semantic attributes, such as gender, body shape, age, area, profession, and clothing—are selected . These attribute values (e.g., male, slim, mid-aged, European, doctor, lab gown) are then filled into a pre-defined textual template that describes the desired image content, specifying both appearance and pose. Finally, we use state-of-the-art text-to-image generation models, including GPT-4o and an text-guided auto-regressive image generation model, Infinity~\cite{infinity}, to enable high-quality, semantically aligned image generation based on these structured prompts.

% In addition, we analyze different text prompts to generate the highest quality input images for texture estimation, thereby ensuring that the generated images exhibit accurate clothing, color, and pose attributes.

\subsection{SMPL/SMPL‑X Fitting Using Human Mesh Recovery}
With the generated front and back images, we apply a state-of-the-art HMR model \cite{stathopoulos2024score} to obtain a pose and shape estimation of the synthetic human in the image, which establishes a precise 2D-to-3D alignment between image pixels and the corresponding regions on the SMPL/SMPL‑X model. An initial SMPL parameter estimate is obtained via a regression network. The SMPL model is then aligned with 2D keypoints by projecting its 3D joints into the image and minimizing the Euclidean distance to the detected landmarks, with additional priors ensuring plausible poses. Metrics such as MPJPE and PA-MPJPE are used to assess the alignment, resulting in a robust 3D human model estimation. This step establishes a reliable one-to-one correspondence between the input image and the 3D body parts, a critical component that many methods bypass by using purely attention-based frameworks. 

\subsection{Inverted Rasterization for Texture Projection}
Our approach then employs an inverted rasterization technique to back‐project pixel colour information from synthesized front and back images into the SMPL model’s unwrapped UV space. In contrast to conventional forward rendering, which maps 3D vertices onto a 2D image, inverted rasterization “lifts” the 2D colour information back onto the corresponding SMPL UV coordinates. Each synthesized view is independently projected, yielding separate UV texture maps. Subsequently, a fusion module integrates these dual‐view projections: in regions where both textures are available, a blending operation enforces spatial coherence, and gaps are filled via a diffusion-based inpainting module. This process produces a unified, high-fidelity texture map that accurately conforms to the underlying 3D geometry.

