\subsection{Text-to-Image Generation Models}

We evaluated two state-of-the-art text-to-image generation systems: (1) OpenAI’s GPT‑4o image generation model and (2) the Infinity model. Using an identical prompt template for both, we generated ten front-and-back image pairs of full-body human subjects. The sample set comprises five everyday outfits (e.g., sportswear, T‑shirts, business attire) and five professional uniforms (patient gown, medical scrubs, police, soldier, firefighter). For each image pair, we recorded three key metrics: (i) output resolution (width×height in pixels), (ii) file size (in megabytes), and (iii) generation latency (in seconds). We then report the average of each metric over all ten pairs.  


\subsection{Texture Estimation}

To quantitatively assess the fidelity of our texture estimation pipeline, we employed two self‐consistency metrics: Mean Projection Angle Error (MPAE) and Overlap Consistency Error (OCE). Both metrics required no external ground‐truth texture and relied solely on the geometry and multi‐view projections produced by our method.

{\bf Mean Projection Angle Error (MPAE)} MPAE measures how well the back‐projected viewing direction aligns with the reconstructed surface normal at each visible texel. Let $\mathcal{P}$ be the set of all texels that receive at least one valid projection. For each texel $i \in \mathcal{P}$, let $\mathbf{n}_i$ be the estimated surface normal and $\mathbf{v}_i$ the normalized viewing vector from the camera to that surface location. The angular deviation $\theta_i$ between $\mathbf{n}_i$ and $\mathbf{v}_i$ is
\begin{equation}
\theta_i = \arccos\bigl(\mathbf{n}_i \cdot \mathbf{v}_i\bigr).
\end{equation}
The MPAE is then defined as the average of these deviations:
\begin{equation}
\mathrm{MPAE} = \frac{1}{|\mathcal{P}|} \sum_{i \in \mathcal{P}} \theta_i.
\end{equation}

{\bf Overlap Consistency Error (OCE)} OCE evaluates the agreement between two views in regions of the texture map that both views cover. Given two views $a$ and $b$, let $\mathcal{O}$ be the set of texels visible in both. For each $i \in \mathcal{O}$, denote by $t_i^a$ and $t_i^b$ the corresponding texture attributes (e.g., intensity or shading response) sampled from views $a$ and $b$, respectively. The Overlap Consistency Error is the mean absolute difference over $\mathcal{O}$:
\begin{equation}
\mathrm{OCE} = \frac{1}{|\mathcal{O}|}
  \sum_{i \in \mathcal{O}}
    \left|\,t_i^a - t_i^b\,\right|.
\end{equation}

