\subsection{Analysis of Text-to-Image Generation Models}  
Table~\ref{tbl:average-output-metrics} reports the average resolution, generation time, and file size for each model over the ten prompts. Both GPT4o and Infinity produce images at 1024×1024 px, ensuring a fair basis for comparison. However, the two models diverge sharply in computational cost and storage footprint. GPT4o requires, on average 33.61 s per dual‐view pair and outputs files of 1.52 MB. Its longer runtime likely reflects more complex internal sampling or larger internal network layers devoted to visual fidelity. Infinity completes each dual‐view generation in 3.60 s while producing much smaller files (0.16 MB). This suggests a markedly more efficient architecture and a higher compression of output images.

\subsection{Analysis of Texture Estimation}  
Table~\ref{tbl:reprojection-error} summarizes two self‑consistency metrics—MPAE and OCE—that quantify the geometric coherence of the estimated textures without requiring ground truth. GPT4o achieves an MPAE of 1.09 and an OCE of 2.72. The lower MPAE indicates that its per‑texel back‑projection angles align more closely with surface normals, and its lower OCE shows tighter agreement between front and back views in overlapping regions. Infinity exhibits a higher MPAE (1.32) and OCE (2.97), implying somewhat less accurate per‑pixel geometry alignment and view‑to‑view consistency. These results suggest that GPT4o’s richer internal representation yields more faithful texture placement, whereas Infinity’s faster inference comes at a modest cost in projection fidelity. Qualitative results are presented in Figure~\ref{fig:qualitative} which illustrates the realistic and high-resolution human texture estimation from input prompts for each configuration.

In summary, GPT4o is preferable when maximal texture fidelity is required and computational resources or time are available. Infinity is the better choice for high‑throughput or resource‑constrained settings where slight reductions in geometric accuracy are acceptable.


\section{Conclusions}
In conclusion, the proposed SMPL-GPTexture framework effectively addresses the challenges of generating high-quality, photorealistic textures for 3D human avatars by leveraging state-of-the-art text-to-image generation models. By facilitating the creation of paired front and back images through natural language prompts only, our pipeline addresses the limitations of learning‑based methods that rely on image input with the associated privacy and ethical concerns. The use of HMR on input images and inverted rasterization ensures that the produced texture maps are both accurate and complete. Experimental results demonstrate that our method achieves excellent geometric coherence and texture fidelity. This advancement provides vital insights for applications in virtual try-on, film animation, and beyond, paving the way for new possibilities in computer vision and graphics.

