%\noindent\textbf{Code Optimizations:}
\subsubsection{Code Optimizations}
Our compiler begins by parsing the IR file and the hardware description, lowering the IR instructions to EFFACT's ISA while maintaining the Static Single Assignment (SSA) form. Then it performs copy propagation and constant propagation to eliminate redundant vector copies across different on-chip SRAMs and reduce constant calculation during execution. Our compiler also employs partial redundancy elimination (PRE) using the algorithm described in  \cite{kennedy_partial_1999,knoop_lazy_1992,briggs1994effective} to eliminate the code redundancy. 
% The compiler also performs dead code elimination, removing instructions whose results are unused by subsequent computations and recursively removing their parent instructions. 
% In particular, store instructions to corresponding memory that is not later accessed are also removed in this manner.
Additionally, our compiler performs computation merge as mentioned in Section \ref{sec:arch} through a peephole optimization pass.
Code optimization is crucial as the automatic IR translator introduces some redundant code. In fully-packed bootstrapping, our code optimizer eliminates 12.9\% of instructions, a task that was previously done manually in prior works\cite{kim_ark_2022,MAD,sharp-kim}. Given that previous designs relied on manual optimizations, we have excluded code optimization from our evaluation.

