\subsection{Streaming Memory Access}\label{sec:stream}
% {\color{red} take BConv or an executed program as a example here? first multiplication result is reused directly in the following multiplication by using streaming pass}

In EFFACT architecture, there is a stream memory controller to manage memory accesses. 
Streaming is a pattern that involves the continuous flow of data through computing units and DRAM.
EFFACT employs partial streaming memory access \cite{somogyi2006spatial,nowatzki2017stream}, in which part of data can flow directly from DRAM to function units upon arrival instead of waiting for the SRAM chunk to be filled. 
It aims to maximize computing resource utilization, reduce memory latencies, and more essentially, relieve the SRAM pressure. 
For stream implementation, a separate FIFO address space is added to efficiently collect data. The memory controller handles concurrent accesses from HBM to the on-chip SRAM or among on-chip SRAM and the aforementioned FIFO space. High parallelism with low overhead can be achieved by fully utilizing the associated resources through arbitration of memory accesses. 

% \begin{figure}[t]
%       \centering
%       \includegraphics[width=0.8\columnwidth]{diagram/stream.pdf}
%       %\vskip -2.5ex
%        \vskip -2ex
%       \caption{An example of streaming data in part of the KeySwitch, (a) non-streaming processing requires the $C_{0,0}$ totally on-chip to start the calculation of iNTT, resulting in one cycle SRAM latency, (b) by applying streaming between the load and iNTT, we merge the operations from two cycles to one cycle (the cycle is an abstraction of the running time).}
%       \label{stream}
%       %\vskip -1.5ex
% \end{figure}
As mentioned in Section \ref{sec:compiler}, load operations or normal functional operations with only one consumer will be processed with instruction merging and are allowed to use streaming memory access. 
The merged instruction will first issue the first operation it merges.
Through streaming, vector data that is returned by the first operation can be directly read into the aforementioned FIFO space one by one and subsequently dispatched straight to the second operation's FU or the DRAM. If there are no other data dependencies, the second operation will execute upon arrival of the data without needing to wait for the preceding operation to complete. The data is processed on arrival, eliminating the requirement of waiting for the on-chip SRAM to fill up, and enabling nearly real-time processing of data, as shown in Figure \ref{finegrain}(d).  
Otherwise, in the case where data will be used by multiple consumers, it is filled into the on-chip SRAM for reuse.% with the address assigned by the compiler a priori.

