\subsection{Scalibility of EFFACT}
To analyze how the computing resources and SRAM capacity impact EFFACT's performance, we create EFFACT-54 with 54-MB SRAM and 4096 multipliers, EFFACT-108 with 108-MB SRAM and 8192 multipliers, and EFFACT-162 with 162-MB SRAM and 12288 multipliers. The performance is evaluated using a cycle-accurate C++ simulator. Figure \ref{fig:scale} shows the results. When both the computing and SRAM resources scale up, EFFACT can directly have better performance since (1) when adding multipliers, the performance of NTT instructions increases linearly, (2) the serial normal MULT and ADD can be faster with the help of NTT units, and (3) larger SRAM incur fewer DRAM fetches, which means fewer memory stalls and more DRAM bandwidth can be provided to streaming optimization. According to the simulation results, EFFACT-108 can outperform ARK and CraterLake in HELR and ResNet. Since Bootstrapping is more memory intensive, EFFACT should have a 162-MB SRAM and 12288 multipliers to catch up with ARK and CraterLake.

