\section{Introduction}
Audio-visual target speaker extraction (AV-TSE) aims to isolate the target speaker's voice from a mixed audio source conditioned on visual cues. This process is inspired by the human selective listening mechanism, which allows the individuals to concentrate on specific speakers in a noisy environment, such as the classic ``cocktail party'' scenario~\cite{brain,select-listen}. 
Currently, most AV-TSE models employ lip movement as visual cues because they are informative, robust to acoustic noises and highly correlated with speech content \cite{muse,av-sepformer,usev,tdse,wu2024target_cvpr,10650095,ImagineNET}.
% According to the speech chain theory, visual cues and auditory signals, although perceived separately, are integrated for extraction and decoding \cite{denes1993speech}. 
% Based on this principle, most current AV-TSE models focus on obtaining efficient audio-visual synchronization cues and exploring different fusion strategies to align them\cite{muse,av-sepformer,usev,tdse,wu2024target_cvpr,wu2024target}. 
Such systems typically focus on obtaining efficient audio-visual synchronization cues and corresponding effective integration methods \cite{muse,av-sepformer,usev,tdse,wu2024target_cvpr,10650095,ImagineNET}, facilitating the alignment of audio-visual information.

% Despite significant progress achieved, some gaps remain between these models and the functionality of the human auditory system.

 % While most AV-TSE models primarily rely on registered acoustic signals and current visual signals for extraction, humans may track the target speaker's voice by predicting their upcoming words or leveraging intermediate memory. 
 % One potential gap between most current AV-TSE system and human brain is the underutilization of contextual information for extraction.
Most current AV-TSE systems primarily rely on the synchronization between audio and visual modalities for extraction. However, beyond such synchronization, the human brain employs additional mechanisms to track a target speaker’s voice, such as predicting upcoming words or recalling prior memories of the speaker. By capturing contextual correlation from the target speaker's utterances, the human brain may focus on the target speaker's voice more effectively while ignoring interfering speakers' utterances.
 % From our daily experience, the former typically occurs in dense multi-speaker conversations, while the latter is common in sparse multi-speaker conversations when humans try to recall a target speaker who spoke earlier. 



% Inspired by this, contextual information can also be important in building the TSE systems, while most current TSE systems primarily utilize signal-level information but underutilize such contextual information\cite{muse, convtas}.
% For AV-TSE models, such contextual information may extend beyond the speech modality, as visual cues can also provide rich inter-modality context. 

% However, such contextual information might not be easily captured from the mixture, and the interfering speech context could even harm extraction performance. 
In previous studies on speech separation, researchers have attempted to leverage contextual information by integrating Automatic Speech Recognition (ASR) modules with separator \cite{Wu2019ImprovingSE, e2easr_separation, li20q_interspeech}. 
However, this approach predominantly captures the content-related information, such as semantics and grammar, while neglecting environmental acoustic context and paralinguistic cues, such as the target speaker's emotion and speech rate,
 which are not visible in ASR transcripts.
% Content information can generally be extracted from transcripts, whereas environmental acoustic and paralinguistic contexts are present in the raw speech signal. 
These forms of contextual information provide valuable cues for target speaker extraction, highlighting the importance of developing robust contextual modeling strategies that can effectively capture and utilize these diverse contexts.
Recently, the Masked Language Modeling (MLM) strategy has gained popularity due to its strong contextual modeling ability \cite{BERT}. Similarly, Masked Acoustic Modeling (MAM) has been effectively applied to many speech foundation models, such as wav2vec 2.0 \cite{wav2vec2}, HuBERT \cite{HuBERT}, and SSAST \cite{Ssast}. Leveraging this self-supervised pre-training strategy, these models produce speech representations enriched with contextual information and have consistently demonstrated superior performance across a wide range of speech-related downstream tasks \cite{wav2vec2, HuBERT}.

