Adoption of FPGAs in clouds has been lackluster due, in part, to the complexity of programming host-managed deployments. In this work, we propose an abstraction for networked accelerators that decouples data transfer from accelerator invocation. Similar to data and control plane separation in modern networks, in \offrac, data transfer happens in a highly efficient streaming manner to FPGAs hosting multiple accelerators, without explicit coordination from a host CPU. Offloading to FPGAs in \offrac is a ``bump in the wire'' operation, that could take place in SmartNICs equipped with FPGAs~\cite{lin2020panic,lin2024supernic,strom}, programmable switches with FPGAs~\cite{aristafpgaswitch,netreduce}, or network-accessible disaggregated FPGAs~\cite{lim2024beehive,maschi2024strega}. After studying the state of the art in acceleration design, we found that when deployed in the data path, stateless accelerators can be highly effective across a range of applications.
% \mcnote{i don't see how this next sentence fits this paragraph.}
